{
  "id": "choose-a-visualization-read-gauges", "title": "Read a value within a range",
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "markdown", "content": "You'll read the range around a gauge's value, then find the same visual idea in a bar gauge." },
    { "type": "section", "id": "read-gauges", "title": "Read ranges and limits", "blocks": [
      { "type": "interactive", "action": "navigate", "reftarget": "/d/3SWXxreWk/grafana-dashboard?viewPanel=22&from=now-1h&to=now&timezone=browser", "verify": "on-page:/d/3SWXxreWk", "content": "Open the **Memory** gauge on **Grafana Dashboard**." },
      { "type": "markdown", "content": "Find the displayed value and follow the filled arc around the gauge. The gauge adds position within a configured range that a value-only stat doesn't show." },
      { "type": "markdown", "content": "Open the panel menu and select **Edit**. Under **Standard options**, read **Min** and **Max**: this example uses 0 and 300. Range endpoints don't have to appear as labels on the gauge, so inspect the settings rather than guessing the limit from its color." },
      { "type": "markdown", "content": "This demo uses a mean and a configured display range, not a verified memory capacity. On your dashboard, a meaningful limit must come from the system or an explicit operational target. Color alone doesn't prove a value is safe or unsafe." },
      { "type": "interactive", "action": "navigate", "reftarget": "/d/3SWXxreWk/grafana-dashboard?viewPanel=28&from=now-1h&to=now&timezone=browser", "verify": "on-page:/d/3SWXxreWk", "content": "Leave the editor without saving and open the horizontal **Google hits** bar gauge." },
      { "type": "markdown", "content": "Find a bar's number and follow its length across the available range. A bar gauge expresses position in a range with a bar instead of an arc. Don't confuse it with a bar chart that compares categories on a shared axis." }
    ] },
    { "type": "markdown", "content": "You've read a gauge and bar gauge as values within configured ranges. Choose them when proximity to a meaningful limit matters, and verify both the limit and the value calculation." }
  ]
}